  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The 3 ways to get a job done</h2>
    <p>For most home projects you can buy the tool, rent it, or hire someone. The cheapest choice depends less on the tool's price than on 4 things the <a href="../../calculators/buy-rent-or-hire.html">buy, rent, or hire calculator</a> asks about.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>How many times you'll use it.</strong> A rental that costs a third of the purchase price wins for one job and loses by the 3rd. Be honest about future projects: a tile saw bought "for the next bathroom" often never sees that bathroom.</li>
      <li><strong>What the tool is worth afterward.</strong> Quality tools resell well. Buying a $600 tool and selling it for $350 after the project costs $250, which may beat a multi-day rental.</li>
      <li><strong>What your time is worth.</strong> A pro might finish in 1 day what takes you 3 weekends. If those hours have real value to you, through paid work or simply time you'd rather spend elsewhere, count them.</li>
      <li><strong>The chance you'll need to redo it.</strong> Mistakes in tile, drywall finishing, or flooring can mean buying materials twice. The less experience you have with a task, the higher this risk, and the more a pro's price includes insurance against it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Don't forget the consumables</h2>
    <p>Rental quotes usually cover the machine, not what it uses up. Floor sanders need sandpaper, tile saws need blades, and pressure washers may need detergent; rental shops often charge for blade wear. Delivery fees, deposits, and the cost of picking the tool up and returning it on time also belong in the comparison.</p>

    <h2>When hiring is the right answer regardless of cost</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Work that requires a permit or license</strong> in your area, which often includes electrical, gas, and structural work.</li>
      <li><strong>Jobs where a mistake is dangerous or hidden,</strong> such as anything involving gas lines, roofing at height, or tree removal near structures.</li>
      <li><strong>Work covered by a warranty or insurance requirement,</strong> where a manufacturer or insurer expects licensed installation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When you do hire, get at least 3 written quotes for the same scope, check licensing and insurance, and avoid paying most of the price up front.</p>
  </div>
</section>
